The commit rules are stated in several places, and the copies
disagree. One asks for a body detailed enough to judge without the
diff, which invites walking through each decision in it; another
forbids the "Prepare for ..." line a prep commit needs. Give the rules
one full statement, next to cli.md and sh.md, drawn from what FreeBSD,
Linux, OpenBSD, PostgreSQL and git.git agree on.

The rules, each with an example from one of those projects:

- One logical change per commit. If the message needs a second
  reason, split the commit.
- The body says why. Its length follows how much there is to judge,
  not the size of the diff. Open with what the code does now, then
  the problem, then the fix in one imperative sentence. A mechanical
  change gets one sentence.
- Land prep commits first, saying what they prepare for. Within a
  posted series a prep commit may refer to a later commit by what it
  does, never by its hash, and never to work outside the series.
- Aim for diffs under about 50 lines; treat 200 as a reason to split
  unless the change is mechanical.
- Tests and docs go in the commit whose behavior they cover.
- How the code works belongs in code comments.
- Summarize list discussion in the message; do not point at it.
